\DOC UNETA_TAC

\TYPE {UNETA_TAC : term -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Given an application term {u x}, rewrites the goal's conclusion so that the
partially applied function {u} appears in explicit eta-expanded form {\x. u x}.

\DESCRIBE
The single {term} argument is an application of a function to a final argument;
its function part identifies the subterm to be eta-expanded, and its final
argument supplies the name of the bound variable used in the resulting
abstraction. The occurrence of the function part {u} in the conclusion is
replaced by {\x. u x}, under the goal's leading universal quantifiers
{x1 ... xn} (where {n} may be zero), which are preserved.
{
      A ?- t
      =============================  UNETA_TAC `u x`
      A ?- t'
}
\noindent where {t'} is {t} with the subterm {u} replaced by {\x. u x}.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this one kind of rewriting, on goals with no
assumptions whose conclusions are universally quantified predicate applications
of partially applied functions.

\ENDDOC
